erwhelmingly by Malays, who were primarily rice cultivators and coastal fishermen, who spoke Malay, which was the lingua franca for most of the island areas of Southeast Asia."<br> McEvedy and Jones (1978) have the population of Malaysia and Singapore 300,000-400,000 over the period 1200-1550 CE.<br> c1500 CE "Melaka then housed an urban population of 100,000, in which eighty different languages were spoken by Malays, Chinese, Arabs, Indians, and other ethnicities."<br> c1400 CE Malacca was a "small fishing settlement".<br>East Coast of Sumatra/Indonesia<br> Hamlets along the coast.'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 969, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 917})
